  "strategy": "Our approach leverages the Fourier-based optimization techniques implemented in the previous program, while introducing more aggressive initialization and exploration strategies to avoid local minima. We focus on three key improvements: subprocess parallelization, automatic hyperparameter tuning, and condition-based algorithm selection for better performance. \n\nThe mathematical formulation remains identical: minimize 2*n*\u2225circular convolution(a,a)\u2225\u221e / (\u2211a)^2. We build on the existing Nesterov GD and LP alternating methods, but now coordinate them better through a master-slave subprocess model. Each subprocess runs up to 25% of the wall-clock time with a fixed 800-length candidate sequence, and we preserve the core function definitions like evaluate_sequence, compute_conv, and solve_conv_lp as building blocks.\n\n1. Initialization: Develop smarter raw seed initialization by combining uniform distribution with adaptive Fourier perturbation, creating candidates that are less likely to be near minima.\n2. Strategy Stages: \n   - Stage 1: Run smart initializations on short time budgets (e.g. 50 seconds) and prepare agents ready for rigorous optimization.\n   - Stage 2: Scale time-to-solution by running optimization in parallel on multiple length scales (800, 600, 1000).\n   - Stage 3: For each length, use closed-form gradient polishing only where the objective gradient isn't trivially available, reducing overhead.\n   - Stage 4: Introduce automatic crossover of good segments between successful optimization runs, spreading value sets more efficiently.\n\nThe resource allocation prioritizes high-value directions:\n- For a fixed length, if optimization progresses beyond a certain score, allocate more time to segment polishing than to full length refits.\n- Dynamically select algorithms (including Fourier-based surrogate structures) based on timescales for different function components, reducing computational friction.\n\nThrough this structure, we aim to achieve a lower objective within the 1500 second budget by injecting randomness strategically and using inter-process seeding to escape local minima.",
